    {"question": "What is testicular cancer?", "ground_truth": "Testicular cancer most often presents as a painless testicular nodule in men aged 20-35, with tumor markers AFP, LDH, and beta-hCG used for diagnosis and monitoring."},
    {"question": "What is thyroid cancer?", "ground_truth": "Thyroid cancer presents as a hard, painless thyroid nodule that does not regress with thyroid hormone; papillary carcinoma has the best prognosis."},
    {"question": "What is bladder cancer?", "ground_truth": "Bladder cancer commonly presents with microscopic or gross hematuria without other symptoms; risk factors include smoking and occupational exposure to aromatic amines."},
    {"question": "What is malignant melanoma?", "ground_truth": "Melanoma is a malignancy of melanocytes with high metastatic potential, with risk factors including fair skin, sun exposure, family history, and dysplastic nevi."},
    {"question": "What is a CNS tumor?", "ground_truth": "Central nervous system tumors present with generalized symptoms like nocturnal headache, seizures, and focal deficits related to tumor location; gliomas comprise about half."},
    {"question": "What is multiple endocrine neoplasia?", "ground_truth": "MEN is a group of inherited syndromes causing hyperplasia or tumors of multiple endocrine glands, including MEN1 (parathyroid, pituitary, pancreas) and MEN2 (thyroid, adrenal)."},

